In this lesson we'll look at how to solve a one-variable linear equation — basically, how to find the value of an unknown letter that makes an equation true. Think of an equation like a set of balanced scales. Whatever operation you do to one side, you have to do the exact same thing to the other side, or the scales tip. So you can add or subtract the same number from both sides, or multiply or divide both sides by the same number, as long as it isn't zero.

Here's a simple two-step recipe. First, undo any addition or subtraction to move the constant away from the variable. Then undo any multiplication or division to get the variable all by itself.

Once you've got an answer, always check it. Just pop your value back into the original equation and see if both sides match. If they do, you've nailed it.

Sometimes equations get trickier, with the variable appearing on both sides or hiding inside brackets. The trick is to expand the brackets first, then gather all the variable terms on one side and the constants on the other, and keep balancing as you go.

